in today's morning rounds a new study  estimates more than one in ten older  Americans binge drink  that's an increase from the previous  decade binge drinking is defined as five  or more drinks at a time for men and  four more drinks at a time for women the  study of this morning determined binge  drinkers 65 and older were more likely  to be men they're also more likely to  use tobacco or cannabis  researchers say the habit can be more  dangerous for older people putting them  at risk for a range of health problems  our dr. Tara Nuala is here to tell us  more doc I I found this really  interesting an estimated 70% of all  hospitalized older persons and up to 50%  of nursing home residents have  alcohol-related problems in general  why is binge drinking specifically  affecting these older Americans well  when we think about binge drinking the  image we usually think about is younger  adults or college students but this is  in fact a growing trend in older  Americans and it's been underreported in  both the scientific literature and in  the media but the problem is that the  alcohol has a very strong effect on  people who are older they're more  vulnerable to the health problems than  even a younger population and when you  look at the percentages as you mentioned  currently around ten point six percent  of older Americans are binge drinkers in  the previous decade it was anywhere from  seven to nine percent so that numbers  growing also growing just general  increased alcohol use and any sort of  harmful alcohol use so is it more  dangerous for older Americans it can be  and there are a couple of reasons why so  they are more sensitive to the alcohol  so they are higher risk of getting  impaired at the same level that might  not impair somebody who's younger many  of them have chronic health problems in  fact in this series twenty to forty  percent of them have things like  diabetes or hypertension or  cardiovascular disease so that can  exacerbate those it can allow them to  develop new problems things like  pancreatitis or gastritis or certain  cancers many of them are on prescription  medications that might interfere and  also it increases the risk of injury or  accidents and Falls and people who  already may have issues with balance or  coordination or reflux and inflammation  and generalized information so are there  guidelines for binge drinking and older  well the recommendations are that really  no American who's older should be  drinking more than three drinks a day  and in fact if you have some sort of  chronic disease or on a prescription  meds should speak to your doctor and  they may recommend something even lower  low-risk drinking is really less than  two drinks a day for a man or one drink  a day for a woman but really important  that doctors really screened for this  and also educate about it because a lot  of these people may be at home drinking  alone nobody really knows what's going  on they the problems may be attributed  to oh they're just getting older that's  why they're more confused and they're  falling so it can be a little bit silent  unless we actually look for it in fact  they said then the metabolism of a  person who's 65 years or older drinking  two or three beers at that age has the  same effect as seven or eight at the age  of twenty right so they're exquisitely  sensitive to it so it's if you what do  you do  very if you have a loved one who's doing  this how do you deal with it that's a  great question because I think a lot of  people tend to shy away from it or they  don't know how to deal with it so and I  think it's important to be compassionate  loving and respectful to these older  Americans who you may feel have a  problem but also you want to be  proactive and so you want to address it  because early intervention is really  essential so you know one of the things  that could be helpful is seeking advice  from a professional can really guide you  towards what type of treatment they  s